<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=sugarcane">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sugarcane</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sugarcane is a tall tropical grass whose stalks are crushed to produce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. It was cultivated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> since ancient times. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> profiles list sugarcane among their agricultural products.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sugarcane.jpg" alt="Sugarcane" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saccharum_officinarum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-125.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hoysala lands joined two economies: the wet Malnad, which produced areca nut, pepper, cardamom and timber, and the drier Maidan plateau, where large irrigation tanks built by kings, nobles and merchants watered rice and <b>sugarcane</b> alongside millet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Mysore's economy rested on agriculture on the dry plateau, where ragi and other millets were the staple crops, and on irrigated rice and <b>sugarcane</b> in the valleys of the Kaveri and its tributaries, watered by an extensive network of tanks and channels maintained by rulers and local notables.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Agriculture was the principal economic activity, with rice grown on the Konkan coast and jowar, bajra, cotton and <b>sugarcane</b> on the Deccan plateau, while the forests of the Western Ghats supplied timber and other products.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The general strike of 1930, a long series of militant stoppages and the first national union of <b>sugarcane</b> workers made Cuban labour by 1933 more highly organised and more radically led than almost any proletariat in Latin America, and the unions later won perhaps the largest privileges in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sikh Empire governed one of the most agriculturally productive regions of the subcontinent, with the five rivers of the Punjab, the Jhelum, Chenab, Ravi, Beas and Sutlej, sustaining intensive cultivation of wheat, cotton and <b>sugarcane</b> across a densely populated agrarian hinterland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire covered the black-soil plateau of the Deccan and the fertile valleys of the Krishna and Tungabhadra, where tanks and canals watered rice, <b>sugarcane</b> and cotton alongside dry crops of millet and pulses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
